# `new RegExp("a+", "g")`, `RegExp("a+")`: both texts are written, so the call is the literal, which has a record and code in the image. The run time parses nothing.
# function made() { return new RegExp("a+", "g"); }
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = NewRegExp pattern="a+" regExpFlags=[Global]
# RUN(NOCALL): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# NOCALL-NOT: CallInline
# NOCALL-NOT: Construct
# function called() { return RegExp("a+"); }
# RUN(CALLED): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CALLED-LABEL: code f2:Body
# CALLED: = NewRegExp pattern="a+"
# The image holds code for it: /a+/ with and without g have one record, /^b$/i another.
# RUN(CODE): compile RegExpsCompiled mechanisms=all
# CODE: statistic RegExpsCompiled 2
# A text that is joined of written texts is written: function joined() { return new RegExp("^" + "b" + "$", "i"); }
# RUN(JOINED): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# JOINED-LABEL: code f3:Body
# JOINED: = NewRegExp pattern=made "^b$" regExpFlags=[IgnoreCase]
# No RegExp can be made of the text: the call stays, and throws where it stands. function broken() { return new RegExp("("); }
# RUN(BROKEN): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# BROKEN-LABEL: code f4:Body
# BROKEN-NOT: NewRegExp pattern
# BROKEN: inlineBuiltin=NewRegExp
# Nor with such flags: function doubled() { return new RegExp("a", "gg"); }
# RUN(DOUBLED): walk f5 mechanisms=all
# DOUBLED-LABEL: code f5:Body
# DOUBLED-NOT: NewRegExp pattern
# Withheld, the construction is made as it is written.
# RUN(WITHHELD): walk f1 mechanisms=all-BuiltinCalls
# WITHHELD-LABEL: code f1:Body
# WITHHELD-NOT: NewRegExp
# WITHHELD: = Construct

